  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY NIGHT THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 215 PM CDT Mon Sep 28 2026\n\nTrough over the eastern CONUS continues to break down through \nTuesday/Wednesday, with the ridge that has been locked in place \noverhead tracking easterly. Upper level flow will then increase out \nof the southwest as a deep trof digs into the Desert Southwest. A \nstrong fetch of moisture in connection with the Eastern Pacific will \nset up, directing an unseasonably moist airmass into the central \nCONUS. This increase in moisture is aided by tropical systems that \nwill get caught in the flow from the Eastern Pacific, serving as a \nmoisture conveyor into the region, with plenty of forcing to favor \nprecipitation chances. Not only will this help supply moisture to \nthe region, but llvl southerly/southwesterly flow will usher Gulf \nmoisture in as well. Thus, an anomalously moist airmass will be in \nplace through mid week, with PWATS between 1.50-2.00\" or higher \nmoving in. As such, the WPC has highlighted our area in a Slight \nRisk, level 2 of 4, for Excessive Rainfall and Flash Flooding\nfor Wednesday and Thursday. The wave digging into the Desert \nSouthwest will slowly traverse over the Rockies, guiding bouts \nof energy towards the Upper Mississippi River Valley, with a \nbaroclinic zone setting up nearby. This baroclinic zone will \nserve as a focus for the potential for rounds of showers/storms,\nwith heavy rainfall possible. \n\nLatest model trends have the heaviest axis of QPF across\nportions of central into eastern Iowa, where some areas could\nreceive over 3\" of rainfall from late Tuesday night through \nThursday night. However, the exact north/south placement is \nuncertain. The EC ensemble mean continues to be the southern \nmost solution (and wettest) with the highest rainfall amounts \nalong the I-80 to Highway 30 corridors. Other ensemble models \n(GEFS/AIGEFS/EC AIFS/CMC) are shifted more to the northwest, \nbut still clip our northwest counties with some of the higher \nrainfall amounts of 2\"+. Given this uncertainty, and since the \nheavy rain will come in several separate waves Wednesday - \nThursday, will hold off on a Flood Watch for now.\n\nThe timing has also trended later over recent forecast runs. We\nwere initially looking at the potential first round of \nshowers/storms moving in Tuesday night, but we are now looking \nat potential onset closer to the early Wednesday AM timeframe. \nThe more widespread rainfall chances will hold off until \nWednesday PM and into Thursday (60-90% chances). Through this \n~2 day stretch, WPC has a widespread 1.75\" to 2.5\"+ across the \noutlook area, with the highest amounts favored west of the \nMississippi River. Certainly, localized higher totals of 3\"+ are\npossible. The latest NBM 48-hr QPF probabilities for 2\"+ are \nfairly high between 50-80% (southeast to northwest). We will \ncontinue to monitor trends in the forecast, especially as more \nhi-res guidance comes in. \n\nLate this week and into the weekend, zonal to northwest flow will \nset up over the region. We might see some weak waves work through \nthe flow, but much uncertainty remains on moisture availability. \nGuidance has trended drier through the weekend than in previous \nruns, with chances between 15-30% at this time.",
